e result in R is an xml_document. In Python, the most natural equivalent \n    for an HTML document object is a BeautifulSoup object.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Construct the minimal HTML string as R does\n    # R: paste0(\"<!doctype html>\\n\", \"<meta charset=utf-8>\\n\", \"<title>\", title, \"</title>\\n\", html)\n    full_html = f\"<!doctype html>\\n<meta charset=utf-8>\\n<title>{title}</title>\\n{html}\"\n    \n    # xml2::read_html parses HTML. BeautifulSoup's 'html.parser' is the standard Python equivalent.\n    # We return the soup object to mirror the 'xml_document' object return.\n    return BeautifulSoup(full_html, 'html.parser')\n", "failures": [{"call": "minimal_html(\"\\n  <ul>\\n    <li><b>C-3PO</b> is a <i>droid</i> that weighs <span class='weight'>167 kg</span></li>\\n    <li><b>R2-D2</b> is a <i>droid</i> that weighs <span class='weight'>96 kg</span></li>\\n    <li><b>Yoda</b> weighs <span class='weight'>66 kg</span></li>\\n    <li><b>R4-P17</b> is a <i>droid</i></li>\\n  </ul>\\n  \")", "expected": {"__r2py_fp__": true, "class": ["xml_document", "xml_node"], "text": "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n<head>\n<meta http-equiv=\"Content-Type\" content=\"text/html; charset=UTF-8\">\n<meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n<title></title>\n</head>\n<body><ul>\n<li>\n<b>C-3PO</b> is a <i>droid</i> that weighs <span class=\"weight\">167 kg</span>\n</li>\n    <li>\n<b>R2-D2</b> is a <i>droid</i> that weighs <span class=\"weight\">96 kg</span>\n</li>\n    <li>\n<b>Yoda</b> weighs <span class=\"weight\">66 kg</span>\n</li>\n    <li>\n<b>R4-P17</b> is a <i>droid</i>\n</li>\n  </ul></body>\n</html>\n"}, "got": {"__r2py_fp__": true, "type": "bs4.BeautifulSoup", "numbers": {".children.gi_code.co_argcount": [1.0], ".children.gi_code.co_firstlineno": [3255.0], ".children.gi_code.co_flags": [16777267.0], ".children.gi_code.co_kwonlyargcount": [0.0], ".children.gi_code.co_nlocals": [2.0], ".children.gi_frame.f_lineno": [3255.0], ".contents[4].sourceline": [3.0], ".contents[6].sourceline": [5.0]